---
description: GitHub closes an issue when a closing verb sits next to its number, including inside a negation
alwaysApply: true
---

# GitHub closing keywords

Merging a pull request completes every issue whose number is preceded by a closing verb in the title, the body, or the squash commit message. The verb is matched as text. A negation still matches.

The verbs are `close`, `closes`, `closed`, `fix`, `fixes`, `fixed`, `resolve`, `resolves`, and `resolved`.

## When the PR finishes the issue

`Closes #N` is the only closing line. It stands alone under `## Related`. The same verb does not appear again next to that number.

The line lives in the pull request body only, never in a commit body. GitHub's default squash message concatenates the commit bodies, so a commit that says it closes an issue completes that issue at merge, whatever the PR body says by then (#138, #139). A commit body names the issue as `#N`.

## When the PR does not finish the issue

Name the issue as `#N` with no closing verb anywhere before that number. Say that the acceptance text is still unmet.

```text
# BAD — #122 merged and completed #120
This does not close #120.

# GOOD — #120 stays open
The acceptance text on #120 is still unmet.
```

Before `gh pr create`, read the title and the body. If a closing verb sits next to an issue you are not finishing, rewrite that sentence. Do not paste the bad example above into a pull request.
